import{_ as t,o as a,c as s,a2 as o}from"./chunks/framework.Cdp8-jB6.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"The Long View: Editorial Voice for Orrery Essays","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guides/essay-voice.md","filePath":"guides/essay-voice.md"}'),r={name:"guides/essay-voice.md"};function n(i,e,h,l,d,c){return a(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<h1 id="the-long-view-editorial-voice-for-orrery-essays" tabindex="-1">The Long View: Editorial Voice for Orrery Essays <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-long-view-editorial-voice-for-orrery-essays" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The Long View: Editorial Voice for Orrery Essays&quot;">​</a></h1><p><em>The anchor for how every essay in <strong>The Long View</strong> (<code>/essays/{slug}</code>) is written. Companion to — and a deliberate fork of — <a href="./programs-editorial-voice">the Field Historian guide</a> that governs <code>/programs</code>. Same DNA; different job. Read this before authoring or regenerating any essay. v0.1 (draft) · July 2026</em></p><blockquote><p>A program page tells the story of <strong>one campaign</strong>. An essay makes an <strong>argument about a whole subject</strong> — navigation, propulsion, reuse, the long history of getting anywhere — cutting across every mission, agency, and decade the atlas holds. If a program page is a biography, an essay is the historian stepping back to explain what the biographies, taken together, actually <em>mean</em>.</p></blockquote><hr><h2 id="preamble-the-same-person-a-harder-assignment" tabindex="-1">Preamble: the same person, a harder assignment <a class="header-anchor" href="#preamble-the-same-person-a-harder-assignment" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Preamble: the same person, a harder assignment&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The essayist <strong>is</strong> the field historian — same rigor, same craft, same banned words, same refusal to cheerlead or moralize. Everything in the <a href="./programs-editorial-voice">Field Historian guide §2–§4</a> still binds: facts carry the weight; adjectives don&#39;t; every number gets its unit; say the uncomfortable thing flatly; anchor the abstract in a real, checkable human detail; hook then momentum; no hype.</p><p>What changes is the <strong>shape of the work</strong>. A program editorial fills a fixed spine (the land → goals → outcome → narrative → legacy → lessons). An essay has <strong>no spine — it has a thesis</strong>, and it earns its structure by pursuing that thesis. It is discursive where a program is reportorial: it is allowed to reason out loud, to draw a line from a 1957 sphere to a 2020 Mars landing, to pose a question in paragraph one and spend two thousand words answering it.</p><p>Three deltas from the program voice, and only three:</p><ol><li><strong>Thesis over chronicle.</strong> Every essay has one load-bearing idea, statable in a sentence, that the reader should carry out the door. <em>&quot;Space has no landmarks, so for seventy years we have navigated by the only fixed thing there is: the sky.&quot;</em> The structure exists to prove that idea, not to cover a topic evenly.</li><li><strong>Connection is the point.</strong> A program page mostly looks inward at its own roster. An essay&#39;s value is <strong>cross-correlation</strong> — it puts <a href="/orrery/docs/missions?id=apollo8">Apollo 8</a>&#39;s sextant next to <a href="/orrery/docs/missions?id=voyager-1">Voyager</a>&#39;s star tracker next to <a href="/orrery/docs/missions?id=perseverance">Perseverance</a>&#39;s terrain camera and shows they&#39;re the same problem, solved three ways, sixty years apart. The atlas is the evidence locker; the essay walks the reader through it.</li><li><strong>&quot;We&quot;, used with intent.</strong> The program voice is third-person by default. The essayist may use <strong>first-person plural</strong> — <em>we</em> learned, <em>we</em> still can&#39;t, <em>we</em> keep re-solving this — as the register of a mind thinking alongside the reader. It&#39;s a shift in stance, not into chattiness: still a note between equals, never a lecture, never a blog&#39;s &quot;so today I want to talk about…&quot;. Zero throat-clearing.</li></ol><p>Substance without craft is a textbook; craft without substance is a blog. The essay is the tension held taut between them — the same balance as the program voice, pulled across a wider span.</p><hr><h2 id="the-cross-link-spine" tabindex="-1">The cross-link spine <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-cross-link-spine" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The cross-link spine&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This is the one mechanic an essay has that a program editorial doesn&#39;t lean on nearly as hard. <strong>The atlas is the essay&#39;s spine.</strong> Where the prose names a mission, a spacecraft, a place, or a concept the atlas already models, it <em>links</em> — inline, in the run of the sentence, never as a footnote or a &quot;see also&quot; dump:</p><ul><li>Missions → <code>/missions?id={id}</code> · fleet → <code>/fleet?id={id}</code></li><li>Science concepts → <code>/science/{tab}/{section}</code> (e.g. <a href="/orrery/docs/science/mission-phases/dead-reckoning">dead reckoning</a>, <a href="/orrery/docs/science/mission-phases/dsn">the Deep Space Network</a>, <a href="/orrery/docs/science/scales-time/frames">reference frames</a>, <a href="/orrery/docs/science/transfers/lambert-problem">the Lambert problem</a>, <a href="/orrery/docs/science/transfers/gravity-assist">gravity assists</a>)</li><li>Interactive surfaces → <code>/explore</code>, <code>/fly</code>, <code>/earth</code>, <code>/moon</code>, <code>/mars</code></li></ul><p>Two rules keep it from becoming a link-farm. <strong>Link the first, best mention, once</strong> — the essay is prose, not a search index; a name links on its first real use and runs plain thereafter. And <strong>every link must be honest</strong>: it goes to a page that genuinely deepens that exact point. A link the reader regrets clicking costs more trust than a missing one.</p><p>The payoff runs both ways. The essay is the connective tissue that makes the atlas feel authored rather than assembled — and where an essay reaches for a concept the atlas <em>doesn&#39;t</em> yet model, that gap is a to-do: a new <a href="/orrery/docs/science"><code>/science</code></a> section, a mission worth adding. Essays are meant to <em>pull</em> the rest of the product forward.</p><hr><h2 id="the-shape-of-an-essay-—-a-scaffold-not-a-cage" tabindex="-1">The shape of an essay — a scaffold, not a cage <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-shape-of-an-essay-—-a-scaffold-not-a-cage" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The shape of an essay — a scaffold, not a cage&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Every essay moves through the same six beats. They are a <strong>scaffold we organize around, not a template we march through</strong> — the seams between them should be invisible, and a given essay can compress two into one or let one run long. But the <em>jobs</em> are non-negotiable, because they&#39;re what a reader unconsciously needs: a reason to start, a reason to trust, the substance, a pivot, something to carry out, and a door to the next thing.</p><ol><li><p><strong>The Way In</strong> — <em>the hook + the promise.</em> The first lines earn the next ten minutes. Open on the vivid problem or the telling image (never a definition), and inside the first breath make the reader feel <strong>why this is worth their time</strong> — the promise of what they&#39;ll understand by the end that they don&#39;t now. This is the &quot;why would I read this&quot; and it has to be unmistakable.</p></li><li><p><strong>The Stakes</strong> — <em>relevance + trust.</em> Widen from the hook to why it matters — what turns on this, why it&#39;s a bigger deal than it looks, why now. And land, early, the one <strong>grounding reference</strong> — the load-bearing fact, number, or frame — that signals <em>we know this cold</em>. That&#39;s the trust beat: a reader who believes we know the subject will follow us anywhere; one who suspects we&#39;re winging it is already gone. (Way In + Stakes together are usually the first two or three paragraphs — the reader should be <em>in</em> by then.)</p></li><li><p><strong>The Body</strong> — <em>the substance, in movements.</em> The explanation: three to six subheaded movements, each a beat in the argument, each <strong>cross-correlating the atlas</strong> (the linked missions/craft/concepts that are the evidence). One continuous line of thought, each section turning the screw one more time. This is the &quot;what is the stuff.&quot;</p></li><li><p><strong>The Turn</strong> — <em>survey becomes point.</em> The pivot where the historical walk-through stops being a walk-through and reveals what it was <em>about</em> all along. The &quot;so <em>that&#39;s</em> the real story&quot; beat. Short; it&#39;s a hinge, not a section.</p></li><li><p><strong>The Takeaway</strong> — <em>spell it out.</em> Don&#39;t be coy and don&#39;t leave it to inference. State plainly the <strong>one thing the reader carries out the door</strong> — the conclusion, the shift in how they&#39;ll see the subject from now on. Not a gimmick or a clever punchline; an earned, clearly-stated payoff that everything above has been building. If a reader can&#39;t say in one sentence what the essay left them with, this beat failed.</p></li><li><p><strong>The Doors</strong> — <em>go further.</em> End by opening outward, never with a tidy bow: <strong>Read next</strong> (two or three sibling essays + atlas destinations) and <strong>Sources</strong> (the load-bearing claims, internal deep-links + outside references, same bar as a program&#39;s <code>links[]</code>). The reader should always finish with somewhere worth clicking.</p></li></ol><h3 id="submerge-the-scaffold-the-most-important-rule-here" tabindex="-1">Submerge the scaffold (the most important rule here) <a class="header-anchor" href="#submerge-the-scaffold-the-most-important-rule-here" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Submerge the scaffold (the most important rule here)&quot;">​</a></h3><p>These six beats are a <strong>skeleton to internalise, never an outline to paste paragraphs onto.</strong> The persona is <em>telling a story</em>, and a story never shows its bones. So:</p><ul><li><strong>No beat is ever announced.</strong> Delete every &quot;here is the takeaway,&quot; &quot;the stakes are,&quot; &quot;so what does this all mean&quot; — the moment the prose narrates its own structure, it reads like a machine executing a formula. The takeaway must land <em>as a realisation the reader arrives at</em>, not as a labelled conclusion.</li><li><strong>Beats bleed.</strong> They are not paragraphs. One beat can be a single sentence; another can run three sections; the Stakes can be braided through the Way In; the Turn can hide inside the last movement. The seams must be invisible.</li><li><strong>Build in slack.</strong> Leave the prose room to breathe, wander a half-step, follow a good tangent — a little looseness is what separates a voice from a template. Rigid one-beat-per-segment writing sounds exactly like what it is.</li><li><strong>The test:</strong> a first-time reader should feel only <em>a good piece of writing</em>. The arc should surface <strong>only on a second read</strong> — <em>&quot;ah, that&#39;s how it was built.&quot;</em> If you can label each paragraph with its beat at a glance, it&#39;s too obedient — loosen it. The scaffold exists to keep us honest about what a reader needs, not to be performed on the page.</li></ul><p>Length: <strong>~1,800–2,800 words.</strong> Long enough to cover ground; short enough to finish in a sitting.</p><hr><h2 id="how-to-use-this" tabindex="-1">How to use this <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-to-use-this" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How to use this&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Prototype first.</strong> The <a href="./../wip/the-long-view-navigation-prototype">Navigation essay</a> is the calibration piece — the reference the way Apollo is for programs. Read it before writing another.</li><li><strong>Reviewing.</strong> Same single-author test as the programs guide: could a paragraph sit inside the Navigation essay without a seam? The banned-word list (Field Historian §3) is the fast first pass. Extra essay tests: <em>Is there one thesis, and does every section serve it? Does it connect at least three atlas entries the reader wouldn&#39;t have connected themselves? Is there a real turn?</em></li><li><strong>Translation.</strong> English source register only; the i18n style guide governs the 14-locale pipeline, same as everywhere.</li></ul><hr><p><em>Orrery · The Long View — essay editorial voice · July 2026 (draft). Fork of the Field Historian guide; Navigation is the reference implementation.</em></p>',29)])])}const u=t(r,[["render",n]]);export{m as __pageData,u as default};
